AB  - This study investigated the relationship between Artificial Intelligence (AI) Literacy and AI Dependence among university students, emphasizing implications for autonomy, professional agency, and the Zone of Proximal Development in the 21st -century formation of future educators. A quantitative cross-sectional design was employed, involving 370 respondents who completed validated instruments assessing AI literacy across cognitive, applied, ethical, and creation dimensions, alongside AI dependence across uncontrollability, withdrawal symptoms, mood modification, and negative impacts. Descriptive results indicated that students demonstrated high AI literacy in knowledge, application, ethics, and critical evaluation, but low literacy in AI creation, while dependence was consistently low across all dimensions. Pearson correlation analysis revealed a strong, significant negative relationship between AI literacy and AI dependence (r = -0.946, p < 0.001), suggesting that higher competence in AI effectively reduces reliance and maladaptive engagement with AI tools. Findings underscore that AI literacy functions as a protective factor, promoting purposeful, ethical, and autonomous use of AI, while minimizing dependence. The study recommends integrating hands-on, creation-focused AI training within teacher education programs to bridge practical skill gaps and prepare future educators for the demands of 21st century.
