AB  - This paper analyses global survey data from 109 countries to examine factors influencing early students' use of ChatGPT. Analysing over 20,000 student responses from developed and developing countries, this study finds that over 70 % of students globally use ChatGPT, with higher usage in developed countries (74 %) compared to developing countries (71 %). Logistic regression reveals key social, economic, and financial factors influencing this adoption. In particular, we find differences in usage patterns across regions and between urban, suburban, and rural areas. In developed countries, ChatGPT use is higher among males, younger students, full-time and postgraduate students, those in applied sciences, and those in online or blended learning. Access to financial aid increases ChatGPT usage in both developed and developing countries, while students with jobs in urban areas of developed countries are less likely to use it. These findings and policy recommendations highlight the role of digital technologies in expanding educational access and driving innovation among young students, a vital resource for future research and innovation.
