AB  - The use of artificial intelligence (AI) tools represented by ChatGPT has the potential to enhance language learners’ experience by providing immediate and comprehensive feedback. However, it remains unclear whether AI-generated feedback in Chinese as a Second Language (CSL) learning can impact learners’ emotions, writing confidence, and writing proficiency. To address this gap, sixteen learners from an advanced CSL intact class at a university in New Zealand participated in this mixed-methods quasi-experimental study. The quantitative findings indicate that (1) paired-samples t-tests revealed significant improvements in both writing confidence and outcomes in the post-writing task compared to the pre-writing task, and (2) correlation analysis showed that improvements in CSL learners’ writing confidence were associated with their attitudes toward AI. The qualitative findings reveal that notable emotional differences emerged across proficiency levels: high-proficiency CSL learners generally reported positive or mixed emotions when using AI for writing support, whereas lower-proficiency learners predominantly experienced negative emotions (e.g., stress, anxiety, and confusion) both before and after the intervention. By integrating AI into CSL pedagogy, this study contributes new insights into fostering learners’ confidence, achievements, and emotions in the contexts of emerging AI technologies.
