AB  - This paper proposed a real-time web application to present the statistical results to explore the student’s demographic impact and association of students’ responses towards ChatGPT content accuracy, inaccuracy, and content validation approaches. This study examined whether demographic factors influence the accuracy of responses generated by ChatGPT. Using contingency table analyses, we evaluated associations between users’ region, gender, and academic class and the correctness of ChatGPT’s responses. The results indicate significant relationships across all three variables. Region was moderately associated with response validation accuracy (χ2= 8.21, p = .004, Cramer’s V = .405), while gender showed an even stronger association (χ2= 11.49, p < .001, Cramer’s V = .479), suggesting notable disparities in how different groups interact with or are interpreted by the model. Academic class also demonstrated a significant, though smaller, effect (χ2= 4.66, p = .031, Cramer’s V = .216). Assumption testing supported the use of non-parametric methods for comparing response accuracy across academic levels, and a Mann-Whitney U test confirmed a significant difference in accurate responses between Bachelor’s and Master’s students (U = 919.50, p = .036, effect size = .274). A parametric independent t-test revealed a significant difference in inaccurate responses between Bachelor’s and Master’s students (t = -2.338, p = .022). The findings show substantial demographic disparities in ChatGPT’s response validation outcomes, emphasizing the need to test AI systems for biases to ensure fair performance across varied user groups.
