AB  - This study examined statistics anxiety as an affective individual difference in higher education by validating the Statistics Anxiety Scale (SAS) and analyzing changes and potential predictors of anxiety across a university statistics course. Two samples of Chilean undergraduates were used: a pilot sample for the validation analyses (n = 411) and an independent sample for the pre–post assessment conducted across a regular academic semester (n = 122). Confirmatory factor analysis supported the original three-factor structure of the SAS, showing excellent fit and high reliability. Pre–post analyses indicated that statistics anxiety showed limited change over one academic semester in this specific instructional context, with only a marginal decrease in examination-related anxiety. Multilevel models showed that gender was the most consistent predictor across all dimensions, while academic programs shaped specific anxiety profiles. Academic performance displayed limited context-dependent associations with anxiety. Overall, the findings are consistent with the view of statistics anxiety as a multidimensional affective construct that varies systematically across students and educational contexts and that is unlikely to be substantially modified by routine course exposure within a single semester.
