KW  - Generative artificial intelligence
KW  - Learning from multiple sources
KW  - Prompting strategies
KW  - Multiple document reading
KW  - Source integration
AB  - Background
As generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) becomes increasingly incorporated in education, research is needed to understand how students use GenAI while learning from multiple sources. GenAI expands these environments by introducing dynamic, prompt-responsive output alongside more stable materials, yet little is known about the prompting strategies students use in such settings or how those strategies relate to the quality of GenAI-assisted written products.
Aims
We aimed to identify the prompting strategies students used when interacting with a GenAI tool during an authentic academic multiple source integration task and to examine how those strategies were associated with the quality of students’ GenAI-assisted essays.
Sample
Participants were 293 undergraduate students in the northeastern United States.
Methods
Students completed a scenario-based integrative essay task using multiple textbook chapters and ChatGPT-3.5. We analyzed students’ conversation logs qualitatively to develop a coding scheme. We then quantified the frequency of each strategy and used multiple regression analyses to examine whether strategy use predicted four dimensions of integration task performance.
Results
Writing improvement was the strongest positive predictor across essay-quality dimensions. More specifically, task-based framing, learner-based generation, linguistic refinement, and referencing were linked to higher scores for some dimensions, whereas monitoring was negatively associated with several dimensions.
Conclusions
The findings suggest that the educational value of GenAI in learning from multiple sources depends not simply on whether students use it, but on how they use it. Prompting strategies appear to be one observable way students strategically engage with GenAI within these environments.
