KW  - LaTeX
KW  - University mathematics student
KW  - UTAUT model
KW  - Professional development
KW  - Ghana
KW  - Mixed-method
AB  - Digital or computer-assisted writing has replaced the old-fashioned pen-and-paper approach in most tertiary institutions. Students in higher education frequently use digital devices for note-taking, which has the potential to impact their academic achievement. Using electronic devices to take notes in class may enhance quality rather than quantity. One of the most widely used tools in mathematics-related fields is LaTeX, an open-source scientific document-preparation system designed for producing high-quality texts with mathematical notation and symbols. Unlike general-purpose word processors, LaTeX enables precise formatting and has become the de facto standard for scholarly writing in STEM fields. However, factors influencing students' initial acceptance of LaTeX are limited in the literature. This study investigated the factors affecting the adoption of LaTeX among mathematics undergraduate learners at Akenten Appiah-Menka University of Skills Training and Entrepreneurial Development (AAMUSTED). Using a concurrent design with 222 participants from a Ghanaian public university, this research adapted the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) framework to explain the factors that may influence students' intention to use LaTeX after engaging in a four-week professional development training. The quantitative data were evaluated using a structural equation model, while the qualitative data were analysed using a deductive thematic analysis approach. Students’ intention to utilise LaTeX was strongly predicted by performance expectancy (PE), social influence (SI), effort expectancy (EE), and facilitating conditions (FC). However, the best predictor of LaTeX intention was EE, followed by SI, PE, and FC. The Qualitative findings revealed that limited institutional support reduced the effectiveness of facilitating conditions. While the findings provide valuable insights into LaTeX adoption in AAMUSTED, they may not be generalisable to all tertiary students. The study recommends that, within comparable environments, LaTeX training should be simplified, institutional support strengthened, and collaborative peer learning fostered to enhance adoption.
